# Tangerine Compiler — LIR (Low-Level IR) module
# Self-hosting compiler written in Tangerine
#
# Audit P1-7 slice: a TRUE low-level IR standing between semantic MIR and
# machine code, so register allocation becomes a standalone pass instead of
# the direct emitter's inline RegAllocState. The direct emitter in
# codegen.tg is untouched; this module is purely additive and default-off
# (see the TANGERINE_LIR gate in driver.tg's compile_lir_route).
#
# Desired end-to-end pipeline (the audit's target):
#   TypedProgram -> Ownership MIR -> semantic optimization -> LIR ->
#   machine legalization -> register allocation -> peephole/scheduling ->
#   object emission.
# This module implements the LIR stage of that pipeline for a CORE aarch64
# integer subset. MIR keeps moves/drops/ownership/typed places/high-level
# aggregates; LIR has virtual registers, basic blocks, load/store, machine-
# width arithmetic, compare/branch, call ABI operands and explicit spill
# slots. Once the safety semantics are proven in MIR, they need no runtime
# representation: drops/ownership are already resolved into MIR deinit
# terms BEFORE this stage — the MirDeinit terminators that arrive lower
# on the aarch64 whole-program route per the MirDeinit slice below — and
# anything outside the supported subset FAILS CLOSED (see the
# legalization contract below; never a silent miscompile).
#
# ── Legalization contract (what lowers, and how) ──────────────────────
#
# SUPPORTED MIR INPUT (the current subset — aarch64 host only):
#   Functions: non-extern bodies whose PARAMETER types are scalar: Unit,
#     Bool, Int, UInt, ISize/USize, the sized integers I8/I16/I32/
#     U8/U16/U32 (the width model below) and the FLOATING-POINT scalars
#     Float/F32/F64 (the float slice below; I64/U64/I128/U128/Char stay
#     outside the slice — fail-closed), or — on the
#     program-level aarch64 route only — an admitted PLAIN STRUCT of
#     scalar words. Its image class decides the crossing: an
#     ADDRESS-class image (exactly 16 bytes (ScalarPair) or more than 8
#     bytes (ByAddress)) consumes ONE ABI arg slot — its GENERAL-class
#     position among all parameters — carrying the ADDRESS of the
#     caller's image (fetched at function entry and copied word-by-word
#     into the parameter's aggregate frame slots: the aggregate-param
#     slice), while a Scalar-class image (<= 8 bytes — every admitted
#     such shape is its ONE word, a single whole-word field) crosses BY
#     VALUE as that word in the parameter's ONE slot and binds at entry
#     into the parameter's fixed word-0 vreg like a scalar word (the
#     register-class slice, the mirror of the payload-less enum
#     parameter below).
#     A PAYLOAD-LESS TAGGED-ENUM parameter also lowers on that route —
#     its raw-discriminant tag word crosses in its ONE slot like a
#     scalar word (the direct pipeline's enum parameters are Scalar(8)
#     value words), bound into the enum local's fixed tag-word slot at
#     entry. A PAYLOADED (payload-carrying) TAGGED-ENUM parameter
#     lowers on that route too, under the LIR-INTERNAL ENUM ABI (a
#     documented divergence — see the crossing rule below): its ONE
#     slot carries the CALLER's inline image address and the entry
#     binding copies the image words into the parameter's frame slots
#     exactly like an address-class plain-struct parameter.
#     RETURN types are those scalars too, or an admitted PLAIN STRUCT of
#     scalar words whose aggregate image classifies exactly as in the
#     direct pipeline (codegen.tg's classify_value_category /
#     category_uses_sret, mirrored on the mirror layout size): a
#     Scalar-class image of <= 8 bytes — every admitted one-word shape —
#     returns as ONE word in x0, and an sret-class image — exactly 16
#     bytes (ScalarPair) or more than 8 bytes (ByAddress) — returns
#     through the caller-supplied x8 pointer. A PAYLOAD-LESS TAGGED-ENUM
#     return (the 8-byte tag-word image) also returns as ONE word in x0
#     — the mirror of the direct pipeline's Scalar(8) enum crossing,
#     whose value word for such an enum IS the raw discriminant; a
#     PAYLOADED (payload-carrying) enum return returns through the same
#     caller-supplied x8 pointer an sret-class plain struct uses — the
#     LIR-internal enum ABI (its direct crossing word would be the
#     TaggedPtr heap-pointer encoding
#     this slice cannot materialize — the enum crossing rule below). BOTH aggregate-return
#     classes are implemented in this module on BOTH sides: the CALLEE
#     side (a Scalar-class return's every Ret loads the return
#     aggregate's single word from its fixed frame slot into x0 exactly
#     like a scalar return, while the sret-return slice saves x8 into a
#     reserved frame slot at the prologue and every Ret copies the
#     return aggregate's words out to [x8]) and the CALLER side (a call
#     whose destination is a whole plain-struct local classifies the
#     destination image the same way: a Scalar-class destination
#     captures x0 after the call into the destination's single word
#     slot — the ordinary scalar dest write — and an sret-class
#     destination forms &dest in x8 immediately before the call and
#     suppresses the post-call capture, the callee's copy-out being the
#     destination's definition; see the aggregate-return slice below;
#     a whole TAGGED-ENUM destination takes the same class by its
#     image — payload-less in x0, payloaded through x8).
#     Aggregate shapes that stay outside the slice, in plain
#     prose: the layouts the shape gate rejects (tuples, fixed arrays,
#     @packed/@align, sub-word fields sharing or straddling an 8-byte
#     word, zero-field structs) never reach any ABI crossing — the
#     whole-word one-word (Scalar-class) and address-class images are
#     the only plain-struct crossings, both implemented on both sides
#     (returns in x0/x8 as above; PARAMETERS either bind through their
#     ABI-slot image address (address class) or by value as the ONE
#     slot's word (Scalar class), and ARGUMENTS either pass the source
#     word-group's address (address class) or the ONE word by value
#     (Scalar class)). TAGGED ENUMS (admitted variants of
#     whole-word scalar payloads — see the aggregate-local section)
#     cross between LIR-COMPILED functions under the LIR-INTERNAL ENUM
#     ABI: the direct pipeline's classifier gives every registered enum
#     ONE Scalar(8) crossing word (the TaggedPtr VALUE encoding — raw
#     discriminant or heap pointer), and this slice mirrors that word
#     exactly for the payload-less form (whole image = the 8-byte tag
#     word: x0 returns like a Scalar-class aggregate, args and params
#     in their ONE slot like a scalar word) while a PAYLOADED enum
#     crosses BY REFERENCE to its inline frame image instead — returns/
#     destinations through the sret x8 copy-out, parameters/arguments
#     through the image address in the parameter/argument's ONE slot —
#     the route being whole-program and self-contained, both sides
#     agree by construction (locals, construction, discriminant reads,
#     downcast payload access and whole-value
#     copies of payloaded enums lower inside a function). Only a
#     crossing whose counterpart follows the DIRECT value-word
#     convention — a call to an EXTERN symbol carrying a payloaded
#     enum — fails closed with the lir_enum_crossing_rule reason (the
#     whole-program entries enforce it: see
#     lir_check_extern_enum_calls). By-value aggregate ARGUMENTS are implemented on the
#     same program-level aarch64 route as the caller-side mirror of the
#     aggregate-param slice: an admitted plain-struct argument (a whole
#     plain-struct local with empty projections, carried by MIR as a
#     MirCallValue::Place — the codegen.tg aggregate-argument rule — or
#     as a place-like MirCallValue::Value operand over the constructed
#     temporary's whole local, whose word group an earlier MirAssign
#     filled; both forms cross through the same source word group)
#     consumes ONE positional ABI slot — its position among ALL call
#     arguments, scalars and aggregates alike (the codegen.tg
#     classify_argument order, the same geometry the parameter side
#     fetches) — and its image class decides the crossing: an
#     ADDRESS-class image has the slot carry the ADDRESS of the source
#     word-group's first frame slot ([FP - 8 - 8*base_slot]; the source
#     local is stack-homed by the fixed-slot rule): x_slot for slots
#     0..7, the caller's 16-byte stack word beyond for slots >= 8,
#     formed at the call site by the emitter right before the branch
#     (the sret x8 lea ordering), while a one-word (Scalar-class)
#     argument — the caller of a one-word plain-struct parameter, which
#     crosses by value as its one word in the slot register — passes
#     that word through the ordinary scalar-argument move path (the
#     register-class rule, the caller-side mirror of the register-class
#     parameter binding). An
#     aggregate VALUE argument that does NOT reference a whole aggregate
#     local (a constructed temporary always sits in one — construction
#     lowers into a whole-local word group before the call) fails
#     closed defensively. (A payload-less enum ARGUMENT instead crosses
#     BY VALUE in its one slot — the tag word — the caller-side mirror
#     of the payload-less enum parameter binding; a PAYLOADED enum
#     ARGUMENT crosses BY REFERENCE — its ONE slot carries the source
#     image group's frame address, the caller-side mirror of the
#     payloaded-enum parameter binding.)
#     Local types may be any of those scalars OR a
#     PLAIN STRUCT of scalar words OR an admitted TAGGED ENUM (the
#     aggregate slice — see the
#     aggregate-local section below): every scalar local maps 1:1 to a
#     virtual register; every local whose value may need memory (spill
#     segments, live-across-a-call values) is also assigned an 8-byte
#     FP-relative spill slot by the allocator. Aggregate locals are
#     pure memory: each occupies consecutive 8-byte frame slots (one
#     full word per field/image word) and is never register-resident (the
#     "fixed-slot" rule — see lir_alloc_registers_mode).
#
#   WIDTH MODEL (the sized-integer rule, mirrored from the direct
#     emitter): every scalar value, whatever its MIR type, is a FULL
#     64-bit machine word in virtual registers, spill slots and ABI
#     registers, exactly like
#     codegen.tg (load_place/store_place/allocate_locals keep narrow
#     locals in full 8-byte slots; sized loads/stores exist only at
#     aggregate-field memory — the aggregate slice below keeps every
#     accepted field on its OWN 8-byte word, accessed as a full word, so
#     no sized field access exists here either). The MIR width
#     therefore changes NOTHING in the register/slot mechanics; it is
#     enforced at the slice's width boundaries, mirroring codegen:
#       - IntToInt casts whose TARGET is narrower than 64 bits lower as
#         the canonical narrowing AND-mask (codegen's CastKind::IntToInt:
#         and dst, src, (1<<bits)-1 — the low bits are kept, the upper
#         bits zeroed); same-width casts and casts to the 64-bit targets
#         (Int/UInt/ISize/USize) are plain full-word moves — widening is
#         a register no-op in the direct emitter, so no sign/zero
#         extension is ever inserted at a cast;
#       - signedness (I8/I16/I32/ISize/Int signed; U8/U16/U32/USize/
#         UInt unsigned) drives the Shr/Div op choice and the comparison
#         condition exactly as the Int/UInt rule always did; the machine
#         ops run over the full words;
#       - narrow binop results are NOT re-truncated at the store: the
#         direct emitter writes the full word into a narrow local's
#         8-byte slot. Where the language needs truncation the MIR
#         carries the canonical narrowing cast (the P1-5 checked-op
#         chains end with one), which lowers through the mask rule above.
#         The P1-5 checked Add/Sub/Mul chains (small-width signed) mask
#         and sign-extend their operands IN MIR, assert overflow on the
#         SIGNED width BEFORE LIR sees the result, and deliver through
#         the narrowing cast — so the LIR truncation matches the direct
#         emitter and no double check is needed;
#       - the signed-range comparisons/casts inside those chains imply
#         NO extra width ops; the only new statement shapes are the Bool
#         forms the chains emit (below).
#   Bool values are 0/1 held in a full word, and Bool words may cross
#     the call ABI like any other scalar (0/1 in x0-x7/stack slots —
#     codegen's Bool convention). The checked-chain Bool shapes lower:
#     Eq/Ne and BitAnd/BitOr/BitXor over Bool words (plain word compares
#     and logicals) plus Not over Bool (codegen's test/setcc-eq mirror:
#     cmp 0, cset eq). Bool still never appears in integer arithmetic.
#
#   THE FLOAT SLICE (Float/F32/F64 — the floating-point register
#     class): every float value lives in the SIMD&FP register file or
#     in an 8-byte-indexed spill slot, in CANONICAL width form:
#       - F64 (and the legacy Float spelling — this compiler's
#         double) values ride D registers holding the double's bit
#         pattern, and spill slots hold the full 8 bytes (str/ldr d);
#       - F32 values ride S registers in canonical single form (the
#         low 32 bits) — every F32 op is an S-form instruction that
#         rounds EXACTLY ONCE to single precision under the default
#         FPCR (round-to-nearest-even), so F32 arithmetic has true
#         IEEE single semantics, never double-math-on-single-data —
#         and F32 spill slots hold the low 4 bytes (str/ldr s; the
#         slot's upper bytes are never read);
#       - float CONSTANTS: MirFloat carries the compiler's host
#         double. An F64/Float context materializes its bit pattern
#         exactly (fmov d, x — codegen.tg's mov/store/load
#         materialization compressed); an F32 context rounds it to the
#         canonical single at materialization (fcvt s, d — the same
#         instruction the casts use, and the only host-side-free way
#         to round a constant; the compiler performs no float math of
#         its own);
#       - strict IEEE semantics under the P1-6 STRICT spec (types.tg):
#         NaN-ordered comparisons (FCMP under EQ/NE/MI/LS/GT/GE — the
#         exact codegen.tg condition set), signed zero preserved,
#         subnormals preserved, no contraction/reassociation, RNE.
#         @fp(fast) functions (MirFunction.fp_fast) emit the IDENTICAL
#         instruction stream — the mode is recorded per function for
#         the future fast paths, exactly mirroring codegen.tg's
#         current float emission (its P1-6 fast paths are also
#         unimplemented; codegen still emits plain fdiv under fast
#         mode, and so does this slice);
#       - int<->float casts (the MirCast arms below) use the
#         codegen.tg aarch64 instruction choices width-corrected:
#         IntToFloat = SCVTF/UCVTF (S/D by the target width — codegen
#         emits the D form), FloatToInt = FCVTZS (the strict spec's
#         trap-free truncation: toward zero, NaN -> 0, out-of-range
#         saturates), FloatToFloat = FCVT (s2d exact widening / d2s
#         exactly-once rounding — codegen's width-blind no-op is not
#         representable in canonical-width registers, so this slice
#         emits the conversion the spec requires).
#   Statements: MirStorageLive/MirStorageDead/MirNop (no-ops); MirAssign
#     with rvalues: MirMove over a scalar local/constant, MirBinOp over
#     integer operands of any supported width (Add/Sub/Mul/Div/Mod/
#     BitAnd/BitOr/BitXor/Shl/Shr; the comparisons Eq/Ne/Lt/Le/Gt/Ge
#     produce Bool locals and also lower over Bool operands — the
#     checked-chain shapes) or over FLOAT operands (the float slice:
#     Add/Sub/Mul/Div and the six comparisons — see the FLOATING-POINT
#     STATEMENTS paragraph below), MirUnOp (Neg on integer words; BitNot
#     on
#     integer words; Not on Bool; Neg on float words — FNEG), MirCast
#     IntToInt (all in-slice width
#     forms — the mask rule above) and the float cast kinds
#     IntToFloat/FloatToInt/FloatToFloat (the float slice), MirPhi
#     (handled as predecessor-edge
#     copies — see below).
#   AGGREGATE SLICE statements (whole-aggregate values never ride a
#     register; they are memory-to-memory):
#     - MirAssign into a WHOLE plain-struct local with rvalue
#       MirAggregate(Struct(tid, names)): each literal field operand is
#       lowered per its field type (scalar word) and stored into the
#       field's own frame slot — the aggregate is fully initialized by
#       its (possibly reordered) literal;
#     - MirAssign into a WHOLE tagged-enum local with rvalue
#       MirAggregate(Struct(enum_tid, ...)): the ENUM construction —
#       the aggregate's first operand is the constructed variant's raw
#       discriminant (a MirInt constant the MIR lowering wrote),
#       stored into the image's TAG word at byte 0, and each remaining
#       operand is one payload field of the constructed variant (in
#       declaration order — enum payloads carry no names), stored into
#       the payload field's word at its direct-layout offset (the
#       mirror of codegen_aggregate's enum arm, which resolves the
#       variant by discriminant and stores payload operand k at the
#       variant's k-th payload offset);
#     - MirAssign of a whole plain-struct/enum local from MirMove over a
#       whole aggregate place (MirCopy/MirMovePlace/MirRead/
#       MirConsume): the N image words are copied slot-by-slot, one
#       LirMove per word between the two locals' fixed word vregs
#       (mirroring the direct emitter's aggregate copy loop over the
#       layout size; a whole-enum copy moves the tag word and every
#       payload word together);
#     - scalar FIELD places `local.field` (a single Field projection)
#       read and write the field's fixed word vreg exactly like a scalar
#       local: any scalar rvalue shape, operand use, call argument,
#       switch/assert operand, and call destination lower unchanged;
#     - enum DISCRIMINANT reads (MirDiscriminant over a whole enum
#       local) load the image's tag word — construction stored the raw
#       discriminant there, so no register-encoding normalization
#       exists (the direct emitter's load-then-normalize belongs to its
#       TaggedPtr value words);
#     - enum DOWNCAST payload places `local.Variant(v).Field(f)` — the
#       match-arm binding shape — read and write the payload field's
#       word vreg with NO runtime tag check, exactly like the direct
#       emitter's static downcast addressing (the active-variant
#       correspondence is MIR's match guarantee).
#     Every aggregate statement above is aarch64-route-only (the thumb/
#     rv routes keep the fail-closed rejection).
#     FLOATING-POINT statements (the float slice — the FLOAT/F32/F64
#     values described in the width-model/float-slice sections below)
#     lower on the aarch64 route and — in their register-resident
#     envelope — on the cortex-m route
#     (backend_thumb.tg's float arms; the cortex-m float slice admits
#     float locals since the float-body slice and float parameters/
#     returns since the float-ABI slice of that route — see
#     lower_mir_fn_to_lir_cm; the call-argument and emitter halves of
#     the cortex-m crossings are the next slice): MirAssign of MirFloat
#     constants
#     and float place moves (LirFMove), the four float arithmetic ops
#     Add/Sub/Mul/Div (LirFBinop FADD/FSUB/FMUL/FDIV s/d — IEEE-754,
#     the P1-6 STRICT spec: exactly-once rounding, no contraction, no
#     reassociation, subnormals preserved, RNE default; @fp(fast)
#     functions emit the identical stream — the per-function mode is
#     recorded, exactly as codegen.tg records ctx.current_fp_fast with
#     its fast paths unimplemented), float negation (MirUnOp Neg ->
#     FNEG), the six float comparisons Eq..Ge (LirFCmp + the Cset of
#     the signed condition table — ordered IEEE semantics: a NaN
#     operand makes every comparison except != false), int<->float
#     casts (MirCast IntToFloat/FloatToInt/FloatToFloat -> SCVTF/
#     UCVTF/FCVTZS/FCVT — the strict spec's trap-free truncation rule
#     for float->int: toward zero, NaN -> 0, out-of-range saturates),
#     and Transmute — the generic cast kind every source `as` cast
#     lowers to — reinterprets exact-width scalar pairs as the marked
#     register-class bit moves (LirStmt.bm — fmov d/x, and the F32 s/w
#     forms: an F32 word is the low-32 canonical image, so the 32-bit
#     moves transfer it exactly; see LirBitMoveOp): int<->float pairs
#     of matching width (Int/UInt/ISize/USize <-> Float/F64 and
#     I32/U32 <-> F32) plus the same-class same-width word moves, all
#     on the aarch64 route.
#     Float Mod and float bitwise/shift ops stay fail-closed — and no
#     MIR producer can emit them anyway: the type checker types `%`
#     and the bitwise/shift tokens as integer-word-only, so no float
#     Mod or float bitwise/shift expression ever survives checking.
#     Float
#     locals/params/returns ride the SIMD&FP register file (the float
#     slice's own class — see the register model), and float
#     comparisons produce Bool words like every other comparison.
#     Div/Mod lower on the AARCH64 route only (the thumb/rv routes keep
#     the fail-closed rejection — see lower_mir_fn_to_lir_a64): quotient =
#     sdiv/udiv by operand signedness, remainder = num - q*den emitted as
#     a mul/sub pair the emitter fuses into MSUB (codegen's emit_smod
#     shape). The front end emits NO division-by-zero/MIN/-1 guard for
#     source `/` `%` (the signed checked set is Add/Sub/Mul), so a / 0 and
#     MIN / -1 follow the direct emitter's aarch64 semantics (architectural
#     sdiv results: quotient 0 / MIN — x86 idiv's trap is a host
#     divergence this aarch64 slice does not reproduce); a MirAssert guard
#     in the MIR, when present, traps BEFORE the division (see below).
#   Terminators: MirGoto, MirReturn, MirUnreachable/MirAbort (trap),
#     MirSwitchInt over a scalar local, MirAssert (ALL AssertMessage
#     variants — division-by-zero/remainder-by-zero guards, overflow and
#     bounds checks, contract asserts: the condition's truth value
#     (nonzero == true) matching `expected` continues at the target,
#     failure branches to the function's synthetic trap block, mirroring
#     the direct emitter's test/jcc/udf — see lir_lower_assert),
#     MirCall with a direct MirFnItem callee and scalar args of any
#     supported width (aarch64 x0-x7; overflow args on the caller stack
#     in 16-byte slots — the codegen.tg convention) — plus, on the
#     program-level aarch64 route only, admitted by-reference aggregate
#     ARGS (MirCallValue::Place over a whole aggregate local, or a
#     place-like Value operand over a constructed temporary's whole
#     local: address-class plain-struct images AND payloaded tagged-enum
#     images under the LIR-internal enum ABI, one positional ABI slot
#     each carrying the source word-group's
#     address — the aggregate call ABI; see the aggregate-argument slice
#     in the backlog note below — plus payload-less tagged-enum
#     arguments AND one-word Scalar-class plain-struct arguments,
#     crossing by value in their ONE slot (a one-word image whose word
#     is a FLOAT field crosses that word on the float stream at the
#     field's width — see the float-field slice). The cortex-m route
#     (the AAPCS32 parameter-fetch mode, lower_mir_fn_to_lir_cm — see
#     the LIR OUTPUT note below) passes general args 0..3 in r0..r3 and
#     pushes
#     every general arg beyond as a 4-byte AAPCS caller word in order
#     (the first at
#     the lowest address), so functions and calls with more than 4
#     general parameters/arguments lower there too. unwind None, no
#     intrinsic. Scalar args/results cross the INTERNAL call ABI as full
#     machine words (64-bit on the aarch64 route, 32-bit on the
#     cortex-m route; the direct emitter's binding/move path never
#     narrows them); FLOAT args/results cross in the SIMD&FP ABI
#     registers (d0..d7/s0..s7 by float position — see the aarch64 ABI
#     conventions note below; the F32 results return in s0, F64 in d0).
#     On the cortex-m route the float-ABI slice lowers float
#     parameters/returns the same way (the fixed d0..d7 positions
#     under AAPCS32, float params beyond the file on the shared
#     caller-stack stream — see lower_mir_fn_to_lir_cm); its float
#     call-ARGUMENT side is the next slice.
#     The extern-C return normalization codegen applies
#     at its extern boundary (I32/U32 dests) is not modeled by this
#     route.
#     MirReturn of an aggregate RETURN local (locals[0]) lowers through
#     the aggregate-return slice (the sret-return slice section below)
#     by the return image's class: a Scalar-class (<= 8-byte, one-word)
#     image's every Ret loads the return word's fixed frame slot into
#     x0 exactly like a scalar return (a return word that carries a
#     FLOAT field's fp record loads at the field's width into V0
#     instead — the float-field slice's float-stream crossing, with
#     the caller's capture mirroring it), and an sret-class (16-byte /
#     > 8-byte) image's every def stores to the fixed frame slots while
#     each Ret copies the words out to the caller-supplied x8 buffer.
#     MirCall INTO an aggregate destination classifies the destination
#     the same way: a one-word Scalar-class destination captures x0
#     after the call into its single word slot (the scalar dest-capture
#     flow; a float-field one-word destination captures V0 at the
#     field's width), and an sret-class destination lowers through the
#     same slice's CALLER side (x8 = &dest right before the call; no
#     post-call scalar capture — the callee's copy-out defined the
#     destination). By-value
#     aggregate PARAMETERS (address-class images — 16-byte ScalarPair /
#     ByAddress over 8 bytes, plus payloaded tagged-enum images under
#     the LIR-internal enum ABI — admitted by the shape gate on the
#     program-level route) bind at function entry through the
#     aggregate-param slice: the emitter copies each word of the
#     caller's image, addressed through the parameter's ONE ABI arg
#     slot, into the parameter aggregate's fixed frame slots, so the
#     parameter behaves exactly like an aggregate local afterwards (the
#     machine-level copy mirrors the sret slice's X16/X17 load-store
#     pattern; see the entry emission in lir_emit_fn).
#
#   Phi: MIR is not SSA (the convert_to_ssa pass is dormant; nothing in
#     the active pipeline emits MirPhi). MirPhi rvalues in a successor
#     block are lowered as unconditional parallel MOVES at the END of the
#     predecessor (the codegen.tg emit_phi_copies convention: edge copies
#     run before the terminator), cycle-safe via one scratch virtual
#     register.
#
# LIR OUTPUT:
#   LirFunction: virtual-register count (vregs 0..7 are FIXED precolored
#     ABI GENERAL registers x0..x7 on the aarch64 mode / r0..r3 on the
#     cortex-m mode, and vregs 8..15 the fixed ABI FLOAT registers
#     d0..d7 on the aarch64 mode AND the cortex-m mode — never
#     interval-allocated, only read
#     at parameter binding
#     and written by call-argument moves at the LIR level — the aarch64
#     emitter additionally writes fixed registers at MACHINE level for
#     the caller-side sret x8 buffer and the ABI-register-slot
#     aggregate-argument addresses (the address-class images in slots
#     0..7), LIR-invisible writes of the same class as the
#     aggregate-param entry copy; the cortex-m lowering binds
#     general parameters and arguments >= 4 to the AAPCS caller stack,
#     so fixed
#     vregs 4..7 never appear in its LIR), params (one virtual register
#     per SCALAR parameter — ADDRESS-class aggregate parameters have no
#     vreg and bind
#     at machine level through LirFunction.agg_params, the
#     aggregate-param slice; one-word (Scalar-class) plain-struct
#     parameters AND PAYLOAD-LESS tagged-enum parameters bind
#     their single crossing word into the parameter aggregate's fixed
#     word-0 vreg through the entry STATEMENTS (never a machine-level
#     record — the register-class binds of the entry walk), and PAYLOADED tagged-enum parameters bind at machine
#     level through LirFunction.agg_params like address-class
#     plain-struct parameters (the LIR-internal enum ABI); the ABI class of a listed parameter derives
#     from the per-class walk AND the route mode fixed at lowering —
#     aarch64
#     mode: general words in the x0..x7
#     register class by GP position and stack class at [FP+16+16*stk],
#     floats in the d0..d7 register class by FP position; cortex-m
#     mode: general words in the r0..r3
#     register class by GP position and the AAPCS stack class at
#     [FP+8+4*stk],
#     floats in the d0..d7 register class by FP position (0..7) and the
#     width-aware AAPCS float offsets of the shared stack stream beyond
#     (see the entry-binding walk in lower_mir_fn_to_lir_mode_ty) —
#     each fetched at function entry by a LirMove from the fixed ABI
#     vreg / a LirLoadFrame into the
#     parameter's ordinary vreg), blocks, entry block,
#     fp_widths (the float class table — see the register model),
#     statements: Move /
#     Binop / Unop / Cmp / Cset /
#     frame loads (stack parameters — the same LirLoadFrame statement
#     shape, route-correct offset payloads) / caller stack-argument
#     pushes (the same LirPushArg statement shape: a 16-byte caller
#     slot on the aarch64 mode, a 4-byte AAPCS word on the cortex-m
#     mode), the whole-program static-value load (LirStaticLoad — the
#     MirStaticRef lowering of the statics section below: the static's
#     machine word, loaded from its .data/.bss symbol through each
#     route's address-relocation pair), plus the float statement kinds (LirFMove / LirFBinop /
#     LirFNeg / LirFCmp / LirFConst / LirIntToFloat / LirFloatToInt /
#     LirFcvt — aarch64-route and cortex-m-route statements (the
#     cortex-m float slice emits them register-resident only), never
#     riscv-route, like
#     the aarch64-slice division marker on LirStmt (sdiv/udiv — the
#     thumb/rv emitters never receive a DIVISION marker because their
#     lowering route rejects Div/Mod; the POINTER/ATOMIC markers of
#     LirStmt.ptr DO reach the cortex-m and riscv routes once their
#     atomic surfaces open — see the LirPtrOp section). MirAssert failure edges lower to a
#     one-arm zero-test LirSwitch whose trap side (arm or default) is a
#     per-function synthetic trap block (appended after the MIR blocks;
#     term LirTrap), so a trap destination is always a real block.
#     Terminators: Jump / Switch / Ret / Trap / Call (stack_args counts
#     16-byte caller slots on the aarch64 mode and 4-byte caller words —
#     always even — on the cortex-m mode). Spill slots are
#     per-virtual-register homes chosen by the register allocator; the
#     FLOAT slice's spill slots hold the value's width image (8 bytes
#     for F64, the low 4 for an F32 canonical single), indexed in the
#     route's slot region — uniform 8-byte units on the aarch64 route
#     (the riscv route reads its per-vreg count at the machine word),
#     4-byte words on the cortex-m route, where the allocator's
#     cm entry (lir_alloc_registers_cm) sizes an F64 image's home to
#     TWO words at an 8-byte-aligned base — and machine width is 64
#     bits for the
#     aarch64 slice and 32 bits on the
#     cortex-m route (its word-only accept set; sized integer values are
#     full words under the width model above).
#
# LIR INVARIANTS (the allocator/emitter are written to preserve them):
#   I1 flag discipline: only LirCmp and LirFCmp write NZCV. Cset and
#     the Switch
#     terminator's compare-and-branch consume the flags of the most
#     recent LirCmp/LirFCmp in program order within the same block. The
#     allocator inserts ONLY flag-neutral code (aarch64 ldr/str/stur/
#     ldur/orr/movz/movk add/sub and the FP ldr/str/fmov forms — none
#     write NZCV), so a compare's consumer
#     is never separated from it by a flag-writing instruction.
#   I2 call clobber: the allocatable pools, split by call behavior —
#     the GENERAL caller-saved pool x9..x14, the GENERAL callee-saved
#     pool x19..x28, and the FLOAT caller-saved pool d16..d29 (both
#     general pools and the float pool are disjoint from
#     the fixed x0..x7/d0..d7 ABI sets, so precoloring can never collide
#     with an
#     allocated value; scratch x15..x17, the FP temps V30/V31 and
#     platform x18 are never
#     allocated). A value live across a call is split at the call; how
#     its segments cross depends on the pool class:
#       - caller-saved (x9..x14 — the default for segments that do not
#         cross a call): a register-resident pre-call segment stores to
#         its spill slot right before the call; a register-resident
#         post-call segment whose first event is a read reloads from that
#         slot at its first use;
#       - callee-saved (x19..x28 — the aarch64 route assigns the WHOLE
#         hull of a call-crossing vreg to ONE callee-saved register, see
#         lir_alloc_registers_mode): the value rides every call in the
#         register the callee preserves, with NO store/reload and NO
#         spill slot; the prologue saves exactly the used callee-saved
#         registers (LirAlloc.cc_used) and the epilogue restores them.
#     The FLOAT slice adds ONE caller-saved FP pool — d16..d29 (see the
#     register model; the callee-saved d8..d15 and the FP temps
#     V30/V31 are never allocated): a call-crossing float value spills
#     to its slot before the call and reloads at its first post-call
#     read, exactly like the x9..x14 class, and no FP register is ever
#     callee-saved (no FP save/restore block exists in any prologue).
#     The cortex-m/riscv backends keep the caller-saved-only contract
#     (their pools are machine callee-saved already and saved
#     unconditionally) — the cortex-m route through lir_alloc_registers_cm
#     (whose width-aware 4-byte-word slot geometry gives an F64 image a
#     two-word home), riscv through lir_alloc_registers.
#   I3 positions: each statement/terminator occupies one linear position
#     per block in function emission order; per-vreg interval hulls are
#     computed over those positions and cut at every call position. The
#     allocator and the emitter walk the function in the SAME order, so
#     decision lookups (the segment containing a position) are always
#     consistent.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED LOWERING: lower_mir_fn_to_lir returns Result::Err with a
#   reason naming the construct and the rule when ANY unsupported
#   construct appears. The driver route surfaces the error; it never
#   silently falls back per function.
#
# ── Not-implemented backlog (each entry states the exact missing rule) ─
#   Plain-struct locals/construction/copies/field access AND tagged-
#   enum locals ARE implemented
#   (the aggregate slice: locals of Type::Adt whose type def is a Struct
#   whose substituted fields are all scalar words, each starting on its
#   own 8-byte boundary, or an Enum whose admitted variant payloads are
#   scalar words at 8-byte-aligned direct-layout offsets — the image is
#   the tag word + the payload union; see the aggregate-local section
#   below). What
#   remains outside the slice, fail-closed with rule-naming errors:
#   - aggregate shapes beyond plain structs of whole-word scalar fields
#     and tagged enums of whole-word scalar payloads — tuples, fixed
#     arrays, nested aggregates, @packed/@align-attributed layouts,
#     sub-word fields/payload fields sharing an 8-byte slot (sized
#     field memory is not implemented), zero-field structs and
#     zero-variant enums;
#   - enum call-boundary crossings of payloaded enums between
#     LIR-compiled functions: IMPLEMENTED — the LIR-INTERNAL ENUM ABI
#     (returns/args/params/destinations all lower on the aggregate
#     machinery above: the payload-less 8-byte tag-word image — whose
#     word IS the raw discriminant, the direct pipeline's register
#     crossing value — crosses by value, and a payloaded image crosses
#     BY REFERENCE to its inline frame image, replacing the direct
#     pipeline's ONE Scalar(8) TaggedPtr VALUE word whose heap-pointer
#     form would need the direct image allocation). What remains
#     outside the slice, fail-closed with the enum crossing rule: a
#     payloaded-enum crossing whose counterpart follows the DIRECT
#     value-word convention — a call to an EXTERN symbol carrying a
#     payloaded enum argument or destination (see
#     lir_check_extern_enum_calls); payloaded-enum LOCALS,
#     construction, discriminant reads, downcast payload access and
#     whole-value copies DO lower;
#   - MirDeinit terminators/destruction chains ARE IMPLEMENTED on the
#     aarch64 whole-program route (the MirDeinit slice — see
#     lir_lower_deinit): a destruction term over an admitted local
#     whose concrete deinit plan destroys nothing (plan absent/Trivial
#     — scalars and plain aggregates without a registered finalizer)
#     lowers as a NO-OP, exactly like the direct emitter, which emits
#     no destruction code for such values; a destruction term over a
#     local whose concrete plan IS a user finalizer (DeinitPlan::Call,
#     or DeinitPlan::UserFinalizer over an empty structural walk — the
#     only non-trivial plans an admitted shape can carry, its fields
#     being slice scalars) lowers as a DIRECT CALL of the finalizer
#     with the local word group's frame address (its walk would run
#     exactly that finalizer and nothing else); the generated drop-glue
#     FUNCTIONS such plans put into MirProgram.functions — the
#     (ptr: Ptr[T], mask: Int) bodies of mir_build_drop_glue_for_type —
#     are themselves ADMITTED and lower on the same route (the
#     generated drop-glue admission: their Ptr-typed head parameter is
#     an opaque address word and their head copy `value = *ptr` binds
#     through the aggregate-param entry copy — see lir_check_fn_shape
#     and lir_resolve_glue_head_bind; their mask-gated walks, whose
#     leaves are those finalizer calls, then compile through the
#     ordinary statement/terminator lowering);
#     every other plan shape (structural field cleanup over owning
#     fields, the String/collection/Box/Rc destructors, recursion-by-
#     symbol) fails closed with the concrete plan rule. The table-less
#     entries (the thumb/rv routes and the single-function aarch64
#     entries, which carry no MirProgram deinit tables) keep the
#     fail-closed rejection;
#   - the sized-integer locals/params/casts width model IS implemented
#     (see the WIDTH MODEL note above). The sized A64
#     ldrb/ldrh/ldr_w/strb/strh/str_w access forms belong with packed
#     aggregate fields; plain locals — and every accepted aggregate
#     field — keep full-word slots exactly like the direct emitter;
#   - the callee-saved pool x19..x28 (with prologue/epilogue
#     save/restore) IS implemented: see the I2 invariant above and
#     lir_alloc_registers_mode. The caller-saved x9..x14 pool and the
#     scratch x15..x17 set stay as they are — a call-crossing vreg whose
#     hull cannot take a callee-saved register falls back to the
#     original spill/reload path, never into scratch;
#   - floats (Float/F32/F64): IMPLEMENTED — the float slice (register
#     model, statements, lowering, allocation over the d16..d29 pool
#     and emission) is described in the width-model/float-slice note
#     below. What remains outside it, fail-closed with rule-naming
#     errors:
#       - float Mod: no fmod instruction rule exists on any route, but
#         NO MIR producer can emit it — the type checker unifies `%`
#         operands with Type::Int (types.tg check_binary_op), so a
#         source float Mod is rejected before MIR exists (only the
#         hand-built wasm/LIR test programs construct one); float
#         bitwise/shift ops likewise never arrive from source (the
#         checker never types a float bitwise/shift expression — the
#         LIR's float binop lowering fails closed on any such op
#         anyway), so neither needs a lowering rule;
#       - FLOAT AGGREGATE FIELDS ARE implemented (the float-field
#         slice — plain-struct fields AND tagged-enum payload fields,
#         see the aggregate-local section): a Float/F32/F64 field
#         occupies its 8-byte word slot with an fp_widths record on
#         the word vreg (F32's 4-byte direct image rides the slot's
#         low half), and the field crossings of the one-word
#         (Scalar-class) float-field struct images take the float
#         stream — both sides agree by construction. Sub-word packing
#         (two F32 fields sharing a word, etc.) still needs sized
#         field memory and stays fail-closed;
#       - float STACK parameters/arguments beyond the eight d0..d7 ABI
#         registers ARE implemented on the aarch64 route (the shared
#         positional caller-stack stream — one 16-byte slot per
#         stacked parameter/argument of either class: the call-
#         argument walk pushes a float beyond d0..d7 like a general
#         overflow word and the entry-binding walk fetches it at its
#         width from [FP + 16 + 16*stk]); the CORTEX-M route, by
#         contrast, fetches float stack parameters from the AAPCS32
#         caller stack since the float-ABI slice — see the entry-
#         binding walk in lower_mir_fn_to_lir_mode_ty — with its
#         caller side (the width-aware AAPCS32 float-stack pushes)
#         still the next slice (rejected at the call-argument walk);
#       - Transmute int<->float (and same-class same-width word
#         transmutes) IS implemented on the aarch64 route as the
#         marked register-class bit moves (LirStmt.bm — fmov d/x and
#         the F32 s/w forms; see LirBitMoveOp); the cortex-m float
#         slice keeps its fail-closed transmute rejection;
#       - float support on
#         the riscv-rv route (its shape gate rejects every float type — a
#         separate task will add it there); the cortex-m route's gate
#         admits float locals since the float-body slice and float
#         parameters/returns since the float-ABI slice, with the call-arg
#         and emitter halves of those crossings still the next slice.
#     @fp(fast) is recorded per
#     function and emits the identical strict stream, mirroring
#     codegen.tg's current behavior;
#   - aggregate returns — BOTH ABI classes ARE implemented on BOTH
#     sides (aarch64 program route), classified exactly as the direct
#     pipeline classifies the return image: a Scalar-class image (<= 8
#     bytes — every admitted one-word shape) returns as ONE word in x0
#     (the callee side: each Ret loads the return aggregate's single
#     word from its fixed frame slot into x0 exactly like a scalar
#     return, no x8 involved; the caller side: a call whose destination
#     is a whole plain-struct local with a Scalar-class image captures
#     x0 after the call into the destination's single word slot — the
#     ordinary scalar dest write — so the destination local is defined
#     exactly like a one-word scalar destination), and the sret
#     (return-through-x8) ABI classes — exactly 16 bytes (the
#     ScalarPair class) or larger than 8 bytes (the ByAddress class;
#     the admitted whole-word shapes realize it from 24 bytes up) —
#     lower on the callee side (the sret-return slice): the
#     function has a frame slot reserved for the caller-supplied x8
#     pointer, the prologue stores x8 into it (codegen.tg's
#     allocate_locals sret_offset rule), and every Ret reloads x8 and
#     copies the return aggregate's word slots out to [x8 + 8*k] before
#     returning without touching x0 (codegen_epilogue's sret arm: the
#     8-byte-chunk copy — the admitted images are whole words, so
#     per-word stores are byte-exact and the 4/2/1-byte tails never
#     fire), and on the caller side (the caller-side slice: an
#     aggregate-return call site whose destination is a whole
#     plain-struct local with empty projections lowers — the emitter
#     materializes &dest (its first word slot, [FP - 8 - 8*base]) in x8
#     right before the call via add/sub of FP, and no post-call
#     destination store runs, because the callee's copy-out wrote the
#     destination through x8). By-value aggregate
#     PARAMETERS ARE implemented too (the aggregate-param slice, same
#     program route and same ScalarPair/ByAddress admission): an
#     ADDRESS-class plain-struct parameter consumes ONE ABI arg slot — its
#     position among all parameters — and the caller's image address
#     arrives in that slot (x_slot for slots 0..7, the caller's stack
#     word at [FP + 16 + 16*(slot-8)] beyond, the scalar-parameter
#     geometry); the entry emission copies each word of the image into
#     the parameter aggregate's fixed frame slots, after which the
#     parameter behaves exactly like an aggregate local. A
#     Scalar-class (<= 8 bytes) plain-struct parameter — every admitted
#     such image is its ONE word — instead crosses BY VALUE as that
#     word in the parameter's slot and binds through the ordinary entry
#     statement into the parameter's fixed word-0 vreg (the
#     register-class parameter slice, the mirror of the payload-less
#     enum parameter). By-value
#     aggregate ARGUMENTS ARE implemented too (the aggregate-argument
#     slice, the CALLER side of the same program route and the same
#     admission — the aggregate call ABI now
#     lowers end to end with the params side): an ADDRESS-class
#     plain-struct
#     argument (a whole plain-struct local with empty projections
#     carried as MirCallValue::Place) consumes ONE positional ABI slot —
#     its position among ALL arguments — and the slot carries the
#     source word-group's frame address: lowering records one
#     LirAggCallArg per such argument on the call's block
#     (LirFunction.agg_call_args, the sret_call_slots pattern), and the
#     call emitter forms the address ([FP - 8 - 8*base_slot]) right
#     before the branch — x_slot for slots 0..7, the reserved 16-byte
#     caller word for slots >= 8 (the slot is reserved by the ordinary
#     LirPushArg statement stream; the emitter overwrites the pushed
#     word with the address at the call site, after the pre-call spill
#     stores, in the sret x8 lea ordering), while a Scalar-class
#     one-word argument crosses BY VALUE as its single word in the ONE
#     slot through the ordinary scalar-argument move path (the
#     register-class argument slice, the caller-side mirror of the
#     register-class parameter binding). TAGGED-ENUM
#     returns/parameters/arguments/destinations ARE implemented on the
#     same route with the same machinery under the LIR-internal enum
#     ABI: the direct pipeline's enum
#     value IS one Scalar(8) word, and for the payload-less form that
#     word is the image's raw-discriminant tag — the x0/Ret-word
#     machinery returns it, the parameter binding walk binds it from
#     the parameter's ONE slot like a scalar word, and the call
#     argument walk moves it into the argument's ONE slot by value;
#     for the PAYLOADED form the whole INLINE image crosses by
#     reference instead — sret-class returns/destinations through the
#     x8 copy-out, parameters/arguments as the caller image's address
#     in the ONE slot (the plain-struct records above, kind-agnostic
#     at the machine level).
#     What remains
#     outside the slice, in plain prose: the aggregate shapes whose
#     LAYOUTS the shape gate rejects (tuples, fixed arrays,
#     @packed/@align, sub-word fields sharing or straddling an 8-byte
#     word, zero-field structs) fail closed at the gate with its
#     layout reason; PAYLOADED-enum crossings to
#     EXTERN symbols (args and destinations — the direct pipeline's
#     TaggedPtr value-word convention applies there) fail closed with
#     the enum
#     crossing rule (lir_enum_crossing_rule) — the aggregate RETURN
#     slice itself
#     has no remaining gap — the register-class (x0) and sret (x8)
#     classes both lower end to end for plain-struct AND tagged-enum
#     images, and the ZeroSized (empty) images
#     codegen never returns are rejected outright by the shape gate;
#   - peephole/scheduling backlog: not implemented — move elimination
#     via subsumption, cmp+branch fusion beyond the zero-test encoders
#     (a64_cbz/a64_cbnz), fallthrough block layout, constant
#     rematerialization;
#   - entry argc/argv capture for "main": not implemented as a
#     self-contained LIR sequence — the route reuses codegen.tg's
#     emit_save_argc_argv ctx helper (see lir_emit_function).
#
# ── aarch64 ABI conventions used (mirroring codegen.tg) ───────────────
#   THE PER-CLASS ABI WALK (the mixed int/float rule — AAPCS64's
#   independent register files): arguments and parameters are
#   classified by class — GENERAL (integer/Bool words and the ADDRESS
#   words of by-value aggregate parameters/arguments) or FLOAT
#   (Float/F32/F64) — and assigned the class's next register with
#   INDEPENDENT positional numbering: general class positions 0..7
#   ride x0..x7, float class positions 0..7 ride the SIMD&FP ABI
#   registers d0..d7 (s0..s7 for F32 values), and an argument of
#   either class whose file is exhausted crosses on the CALLER STACK
#   in a SHARED positional stream (the position among all
#   stack-crossing arguments, in argument order — AAPCS64's stack
#   numbering; the aarch64 mode's caller slots are the route's usual
#   16-byte words). For signatures without float values the walk
#   reduces to the positional rule — general position == argument
#   index — so the pre-float slice's x0..x7/[FP+16+16k] geometry is
#   byte-identical. The caller side (lir_lower_call) and the callee
#   side (the entry-binding walk) compute the SAME walk over the same
#   signature order, so the two ends agree by construction. Float
#   values that exhaust the d-file (a float at float position >= 8)
#   FAIL CLOSED at both ends (the route's 16-byte caller slots have no
#   float-stack rule — a real C callee would read 8-byte AAPCS stack
#   words). FLOAT returns cross in the FP return register: F64/Float
#   in d0, F32 in s0 (every Ret moves the return vreg to V0 at its
#   width, and every call captures the same register into its float
#   destination — AAPCS64's return rule; the direct pipeline carries
#   float bit patterns in its general registers and crosses floats in
#   GP slots — a documented P1-13 extern-ABI gap there — while this
#   slice's values genuinely live in the FP file, so the v-file ABI is
#   the only representable one, and it is exactly what extern C
#   callees (libm) expect on aarch64).
#   Parameters 0..7 (general positions) in x0..x7; parameters beyond at
#   [FP + 16 + 16*stk] in
#   16-byte caller slots (FP = the frame pointer after stp fp,lr).
#   By-value aggregate parameters (address-class images only — the
#   aggregate-param slice; payloaded tagged-enum parameters ride the
#   SAME address geometry under the LIR-internal enum ABI) consume ONE
#   general-class arg slot each,
#   exactly like a
#   general scalar: slot = the parameter's general-class position, and
#   the caller's image ADDRESS arrives in that slot — x_slot for slots
#   0..7, the caller's 16-byte stack slot at [FP + 16 + 16*(slot-8)]
#   beyond (the codegen.tg prologue binds ScalarPair/ByAddress
#   parameters from arg_reg(arch, idx) the same way — identical when
#   the signature carries no floats). A one-word (Scalar-class, <= 8
#   bytes) plain-struct parameter occupies the same ONE general-class
#   slot BY VALUE instead — its single word arrives there exactly like
#   a general scalar's (the register-class slice). The CALLER side
#   of the same convention (the aggregate-argument slice) is the exact
#   mirror: an ADDRESS-class aggregate ARGUMENT occupies the argument's ONE
#   general-class slot and the caller places the address of the SOURCE
#   aggregate's first word slot ([FP - 8 - 8*base_slot]) there — x_slot
#   for slots 0..7, the pushed caller word for stack-class slots — at the call
#   site, while a one-word (Scalar-class) aggregate ARGUMENT places
#   its single word there by value (the ordinary scalar-argument move
#   path). A TAGGED-ENUM parameter/argument occupies the
#   same ONE general-class slot under the LIR-internal enum ABI (the
#   direct pipeline's classifier never gives a registered enum an
#   address class — every enum is Scalar(8), one value word): a
#   PAYLOAD-LESS enum carries the VALUE — the raw-
#   discriminant tag word — in it (the payload-less image IS that
#   word, the register form of the direct encoding), while a PAYLOADED
#   enum carries the caller's inline image ADDRESS — the same
#   by-reference geometry as the address-class aggregate slots above
#   (a payloaded enum's direct value word would be a TaggedPtr heap
#   pointer; the divergence is coherent between LIR-compiled functions
#   and fails closed at the extern boundary —
#   see lir_enum_crossing_rule and lir_check_extern_enum_calls).
#   Return value in x0 — a scalar word, or the single word of a
#   Scalar-class (<= 8-byte) aggregate return (a one-word plain struct
#   OR a payload-less tagged-enum's raw-discriminant tag word), which
#   the callee loads
#   from the return aggregate's fixed frame slot at every Ret exactly
#   like a scalar; FLOAT returns cross in V0 (s0/d0 — see above); the
#   sret (aggregate, 16-byte / > 8-byte) returns —
#   the sret-class plain-struct images AND the payloaded tagged-enum
#   images (the LIR-internal enum ABI) —
#   copy out to the caller-supplied x8 buffer and never write x0 (the
#   caller passes the buffer address in x8; the callee saves it
#   in its own frame and reloads it at every return). Frame:
#   stp fp,lr [sp,#-16]!; mov fp,sp; sub sp,sp,#frame; spill slot i at
#   [FP - 8 - 8*i] (near the top of the frame); the callee-saved save
#   block sits at the BOTTOM — used[k] at [FP - frame_bytes + 8*k], the
#   codegen.tg aarch64 rule (saved[i] at [fp - stack_size + i*8]) — and
#   frame_bytes (16-aligned) covers both. Spill/address scratch is x15;
#   instruction temporaries are x16/x17 and the FP temps V30/V31;
#   x18 is platform-reserved — all
#   outside the allocatable pools, which are exactly the caller-saved
#   x9..x14, the callee-saved x19..x28 and the float pool d16..d29
#   (the callee-saved pool's
#   28-register target; d8..d15 are never allocated). Aggregate locals
#   sit in the same spill-slot
#   area: the aggregate slot region occupies indexes 0..agg_slots-1
#   (assigned at lowering), vreg spill slots follow at agg_slots.. —
#   all at [FP - 8 - 8*i]; a float value's slot image is its width (4
#   bytes for F32, 8 for F64) at the same addresses; an sret function's
#   saved-x8 word is one
#   further slot the allocator reserves after the vreg spill slots.
#   The cortex-m route (backend_thumb.tg, lower_mir_fn_to_lir_cm) does
#   NOT use these conventions: it consumes the same LIR statement
#   shapes under the AAPCS32 parameter-fetch mode — general params/args
#   0..3 in
#   r0..r3 and every general argument beyond as a 4-byte caller word,
#   float params 0..7 in the fixed float vregs 8..15 (d0..d7) and float
#   params beyond on the caller stack at the width-aware AAPCS32 float
#   offsets of the shared stream (see lir_arg_reg_count /
#   lir_stack_arg_offset / lower_mir_fn_to_lir_cm and the entry-binding
#   walk in lower_mir_fn_to_lir_mode_ty); the LIR's machine
#   word is that route's 32-bit word, and the float forms of its shape
#   gate are the cortex-m float slice (fp_ok — float locals since the
#   float-body slice, float parameters/returns since the float-ABI
#   slice). Its spill slots are 4-byte words (slot i at [FP + 4*i] —
#   the thumb backend's thumb_slot_offset): the cm allocator entry
#   (lir_alloc_registers_cm) sizes every needing-slot image at its
#   class — one word for general/F32 values, TWO words at an
#   8-byte-aligned base for an F64 double — and records the per-slot
#   widths in LirAlloc.slot_words, so the float slice's width image has
#   an exact, neighbour-safe home on this route too.

use std::core::{Option, Result, panic}
use std::collections::{Vec, Map, Set}

